\[U^{(1)}_{g(i)g(j)}=G^{\dagger}_{i}(g)U^{(1)}_{ij}G_{j}(g) \tag{101}\]

and, hence, the 'new' chargon Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}_{\psi}[\mathbf{H}_{i},U^{(1)}_{ij}]\) is still invariant under (100). This means that the symmetries and the qualitative form of the bond as well as current patterns discussed above are unaffected by replacing \(U_{ij}=U^{(0)}_{ij}=Z_{ij}\sigma_{0}\) by the, generally non-diagonal, self-consistent solution \(U_{ij}\) obtained via iteration. The off-diagonal components in \(U_{ij}\) should be seen as additional corrections to the energetics of the spin-density-wave analysis of Appendix B and, hence, are expected to only lead to small changes in the phase boundaries in Fig. 2d-f.

## Appendix D Derivation of \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) theory from SU(2) gauge theory

To derive the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) actions for the different phases in Fig. 1b from the SU(2) gauge theory, it is convenient to use the gauge where the Higgs field is given by

\[\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{i}\right\rangle=(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y}}\mathbf{n}_{0}\,\mathbf{\hat{e}} _{z}, \tag{102}\]

_i.e._, the Higgs field has the form of the spin configuration of an antiferromagnet. Choosing the 'antiferromagnetic gauge' in Eq. (102) is possible for all configurations in Eq. (8) or, more generally, in Eq. (100), since the Higgs field transforms under the adjoint representation of SU(2) and \(\left|\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{i}\right\rangle\right|=H_{0}\).

In this gauge, the relation between the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) fields \(z_{i}=(z_{i\uparrow},z_{i\downarrow})\) and the spinons \(R_{i}\) is given by Eq. (10) for all Higgs configurations. This is verified by noting that Eq. (5) with \(\mathbf{\hat{S}}_{i}=(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y}}\mathbf{n}_{i}\) and \(\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{i}}=z_{i}^{\dagger}\mathbf{\sigma}z_{i}\) will hold for \(R_{i}\) given in Eq. (10) if the Higgs field has the form (102). As \(R_{i}\) transforms nontrivially under SU(2) gauge transformations, see Eq. (6), the relation between \(z_{i}\) and \(R_{i}\) is generally different in a different gauge.

Inserting the parameterization (10) into Eq. (102) and writing \(\chi_{ij}^{ss^{\prime}}=(\chi_{ij})_{ss^{\prime}}\) yields the general form

\[\begin{split}\mathcal{S}_{z}=&\int_{0}^{\beta}d \tau\Biggl{[}\sum_{i}\Bigl{(}(\chi_{ii}^{++}-\chi_{ii}^{--})z_{i}^{\dagger} \partial_{\tau}z_{i}+\chi_{ii}^{-+}\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha} \partial_{\tau}z_{i,\beta}-\chi_{ii}^{+-}\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha} ^{*}\partial_{\tau}z_{i,\beta}^{*}\Bigr{)}\\ &-\sum_{i<j}t_{ij}\Bigl{(}(\chi_{ij}^{++}+\chi_{ji}^{--})z_{i}^{ \dagger}z_{j}+(\chi_{ij}^{-+}-\chi_{ji}^{-+})\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i, \alpha}z_{j,\beta}+\text{c.c.}\Bigr{)}\Biggr{]},\end{split} \tag{103}\]

of the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action. We already notice (the lattice form of) the charge-2 terms \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{\alpha}\partial_{\tau}z_{\beta}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{\alpha}\partial_{a}z_{\beta}\), \(a=x,y\), coupling to the Higgs fields \(P\) and \(Q_{a}\) in Eq. (13).

Depending on the symmetries of the chargon Hamiltonian, some of the terms in Eq. (103) have to vanish as we will discuss next. This corresponds to the absence of condensation of one or both of the Higgs fields \(P\) and \(Q_{a}\) in the phases (A), (B), and (D).

Since this has already been discussed for the case of phase (D) in Ref. [54], we focus here on the other three cases. To begin with phase (B), we apply the gauge transformation \(V_{i}=e^{-i\pi\sigma_{y}/4}e^{i\delta\mathbf{K}\cdot\mathbf{r}_{i}\sigma_{x}/2}\), where \(\delta\mathbf{K}=\mathbf{K}-(\pi,\pi)\), to bring the associated Higgs field configuraton in the form of Eq. (102). In the resulting 'antiferromagnetic gauge', the chargon Hamiltonian reads as

\[\mathcal{H}^{(B)}_{\psi}=-\sum_{i<j,s,s^{\prime}}t_{ij}Z_{ij}\psi^{\dagger}_{ i,s}\left(e^{i\delta\mathbf{K}\cdot(\mathbf{r}_{j}-\mathbf{r}_{i})\frac{\sigma_{x}}{2}} \right)_{ss^{\prime}}\psi_{j,s^{\prime}}-\mu\sum_{i,s}\psi^{\dagger}_{i,s} \psi_{i,s}-H_{0}\sum_{i,s}(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y}}s\,\psi^{\dagger}_{i,s}\psi_{i,s}\,. \tag{104}\]

We see that \(\mathbf{K}\neq(\pi,\pi)\), _i.e._, \(\delta\mathbf{K}\neq 0\), is required to have off-diagonal matrix elements in SU(2) space (\(s\neq s^{\prime}\)) in the Hamiltonian. These are necessary for charge-2 terms in the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action as otherwise \(\chi_{ij}^{s,-s}=0\) and, hence, the prefactors of \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha}\partial_{\tau}z_{i,\beta}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha}z_{j,\beta}\) vanish in Eq. (103). Alternatively, this can also be seen by noting that \(R_{i}V_{i}^{\dagger}=R_{i}|_{z_{i}\to z_{i}e^{i\xi}}\) for the global gauge transformation \(V_{i}=e^{-i\xi\sigma_{z}}\) and that all terms in the chargon Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}^{(B)}_{\psi}\) are invariant under this gauge transformation, \(\psi_{i}\to V_{i}\psi_{i}\), except for the contributions of finite \(\delta\mathbf{K}\) to the hopping term. This means that the effective \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action must be invariant under \(z_{i}\to z_{i}e^{i\xi}\) in the limit \(\delta\mathbf{K}=0\), _i.e._, the charge-2 terms can only arise if \(\delta\mathbf{K}\neq 0\).

To see which of the two possible charge-2 terms in Eq. (103) can be non-zero, let us analyze the symmetries of the chargon Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}^{(B)}_{\psi}\) for non-zero \(\delta\mathbf{K}\). We first note that \(\mathcal{H}^{(B)}_{\psi}\) is invariant under \(\psi_{i,s}\to\psi_{i+\mathbf{\hat{e}}_{\mu},-s}\) leading to

\[\chi_{ij}^{ss^{\prime}}=\chi_{i+\mathbf{\hat{e}}_{\mu}j+\mathbf{\hat{e}}_{\mu}}^{-s-s^{ \prime}}=\chi_{i+2\mathbf{\hat{e}}_{\mu}j+2\mathbf{\hat{e}}_{\mu}}^{ss^{\prime}}. \tag{105}\]From this follows

\[\chi_{ii}^{++}-\chi_{ii}^{--}=\chi_{ii}^{++}-\chi_{i+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}i+\hat{\mathbf{ e}}_{\mu}}^{++}=(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y}}\chi_{\tau}. \tag{49}\]

The constant \(\chi_{\tau}\) can be shown to be real: The Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}_{\psi}^{(B)}\) commutes with the antiunitary operator \(\widetilde{\Theta}\) defined by \(\widetilde{\Theta}\psi_{j,s}\widetilde{\Theta}^{\dagger}=i\sigma_{z}\psi_{j,s}\). This implies

\[\chi_{ij}^{ss^{\prime}}=ss^{\prime}\left(\chi_{ij}^{ss^{\prime}}\right)^{*}=ss^ {\prime}\chi_{ji}^{s^{\prime}s}. \tag{50}\]

This not only leads to \(\chi_{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}\), but can also be used to rewrite

\[\chi_{ij}^{++}+\chi_{ji}^{--} =\chi_{ij}^{++}+\chi_{i+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}j+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}}^{++} =\chi_{i-j}^{t}\in\mathbb{R}, \tag{51a}\] \[\chi_{ij}^{-+}-\chi_{ji}^{-+} =\chi_{ij}^{-+}+\chi_{i+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}j+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}}^{ -+} =\chi_{i-j}^{Q}=-\chi_{j-i}^{Q}\in i\,\mathbb{R}, \tag{51b}\]

where we have also taken advantage of Eq. (48).

We finally consider the symmetry of \(\mathcal{H}_{\psi}^{(B)}\) under the unitary transformation \(\psi_{j,s}\to i\sigma_{z}\psi_{-j,s}\) which, together with Eq. (48), leads to

\[\chi_{ii}^{+-}=-\chi_{i-i}^{+-}=-\chi_{ii}^{+-}=0. \tag{52}\]

Consequently, the terms \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha}\partial_{\tau}z_{i,\beta}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{i,\alpha}^{*}\partial_{\tau}z_{i,\beta}^{*}\) are absent in Eq. (47) for phase (B).

Taken together, the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action \(\mathcal{S}_{z}\) in Eq. (47) assumes the form

\[\mathcal{S}_{z}^{(B)}=\int_{0}^{\beta}d\tau\Biggl{[}\sum_{i}(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y }}\chi_{\tau}\,z_{i}^{\dagger}\partial_{\tau}z_{i}-\sum_{i<j}t_{ij}\left(\chi _{i-j}^{t}\,z_{i}^{\dagger}z_{j}+\chi_{i-j}^{Q}\,\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{ i,\alpha}z_{j,\beta}+\text{c.c.}\right)\Biggr{]}. \tag{53}\]

For concreteness, let us focus on nearest-neighbor hopping (\(t=t_{i,i+\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{\mu}}\)) and \(\delta Q_{x}=\delta Q_{y}\) (corresponding to phase (B)\({}_{(k,k)}\)). Using that \(\chi_{\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{x}}^{t,Q}=\chi_{\hat{\mathbf{e}}_{y}}^{t,Q}\equiv i\chi^{Q,t}\), treating the constraint \(z_{i}^{\dagger}z_{i}=1\) on average by introducing the Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda\), and rewriting

\[z_{i\alpha}\sim z_{\alpha}(\mathbf{r}_{i})+(-1)^{i_{x}+i_{y}}\pi_{\alpha}(\mathbf{r}_{ i}), \tag{54}\]

where \(z(\mathbf{r})\) and \(\pi(\mathbf{r})\) are assumed to be slowly varying continuum fields, a gradient expansion of Eq. (53) yields (\(a\) denotes lattice spacing)

\[\begin{split}\mathcal{S}_{z}^{(B)}\sim\int_{0}^{\beta}d\tau\int \frac{d^{2}r}{a^{2}}\Biggl{[}&\chi_{\tau}(z^{\dagger}\partial_{ \tau}\pi+\pi^{\dagger}\partial_{\tau}z)+(\lambda-t\chi^{t})z^{\dagger}z+( \lambda+t\chi^{t})\pi^{\dagger}\pi\\ &+t\chi^{t}a^{2}\sum_{\mu=x,y}(\partial_{\mu}z^{\dagger}) \partial_{\mu}z+2t\chi^{Q}a\sum_{\mu=x,y}(\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}z_{\alpha} \partial_{\mu}z_{\beta}+\text{c.c})\Biggr{]}.\end{split} \tag{55}\]

In Eq. (55) spatial derivatives up to second (zeroth) order of \(z_{\alpha}\) (\(\pi_{\alpha}\)) are kept as these gives rise to the terms of the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action we are interested in. Indeed, integrating out the \(\pi\) field, we recover the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) theory of the main text with Higgs condensates \(\langle Q_{x}\rangle=\langle Q_{y}\rangle\neq 0\) and \(\langle P\rangle=0\).

In a similar way, the remaining phases, (A) and (C), can be analyzed and one finds the \(\mathbb{CP}^{1}\) action with Higgs condensates summarized in Fig. 1b.

